# Spec: <Area name>

Target code: `src/<path>.ts` ／ Tests: `src/<path>.test.ts`
Related requirements: `.kiro/specs/<feature>/requirements.md` (<ids>)

One-paragraph description of what this area guarantees (e.g. an AlwaysValid value object whose
factory is the only construction path, returning a Result rather than throwing).

## Rules

| ID | Rule | intent/defect |
|---|---|---|
| R-1 | `invariant "..."` | intent (req X) |
| R-2 | `policy "..." when <Event>` | intent (req Y) |
| R-3 | `error <Name> "..."` | intent (req Z) |

## Provenance note (what mutation/PBT surfaced)

If a rule was discovered as a test blind spot (a surviving mutant), say so here — it is the most
valuable kind of rule to write down. Example:
- **R-3 was not pinned by any test**: a surviving mutant showed `<behaviour>` was unverified; the
  test below now kills it.

## Traceability (rule -> test [-> model])

```
R-1 -> test: "<the it() description>"            (<file>.test.ts)
R-2 -> test: "<the it() description>"
R-3 -> test: "<the it() description>"            <- was a blind spot
model: N/A (pure input-space invariant)   |   candidate (order/concurrency rule; no model tooling yet)
```

A rule with no reflection is a TODO, not a spec. A test with no rule ID is unmoored — give it one.

## Known residual mutants (equivalent / low-value — intentionally not chased)

List survivors you decided NOT to kill, with the reason (equivalent mutant, brittle string-literal
assertion, etc.), so a future reader does not waste effort. Do not lower the floor to hide them.
